<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">United States Civil Administration of the Ryukyu Islands</h1><p class="st-meta">1945 CE – 1972 CE · capital Naha</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The American administration of Okinawa and the Ryukyu Islands after the Second World War, which kept them as a military base until their return to Japan in 1972.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>American forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okinawa-island">Okinawa</a> in 1945 in one of the bloodiest battles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a> and set up a military government. In 1950 it was replaced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Civil Administration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyu-islands">Ryukyu Islands</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> treaty of 1951 left the islands under American control.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Final authority lay with American officers, from 1957 a High Commissioner, while a local Government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyu-islands">Ryukyu Islands</a>, created in 1952, handled daily affairs. The military seized farmland for bases, provoking mass protests in the 1950s. The US dollar became the currency in 1958. A reversion movement grew, and in 1968 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyuans">Okinawans</a> elected their chief executive, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yara-chobyo">Yara Chobyo</a>, for the first time.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Amami Islands were returned in 1953. Anger erupted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koza-riot">Koza riot</a> of 1970, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okinawa-reversion-agreement">Okinawa Reversion Agreement</a> the remaining islands returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> on 15 May 1972, though major American bases remained.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/united-states-civil-administration-of-the-ryukyu-islands.jpg" alt="United States Civil Administration of the Ryukyu Islands" width="320" height="169"><figcaption>Image: Dbenbenn, Zscout370, Jacobolus, Indolences, Technion. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_States_(Pantone).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1945 CE – 1972 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Naha</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Paul Caraway, Yara Chobyo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ryukyuan religion, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Okinawan, Japanese, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>USCAR, US Military Government of the Ryukyu Islands, American Okinawa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1958&amp;focus=united-states-civil-administration-of-the-ryukyu-islands" aria-label="United States Civil Administration of the Ryukyu Islands on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8056 1205 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where United States Civil Administration of the Ryukyu Islands lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8376 1461L8529 1145M8376 1461L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8529 1145h0M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8376 1461h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8376 1461h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1958&amp;focus=united-states-civil-administration-of-the-ryukyu-islands">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
